<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill of Tara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hill of Tara is a low ridge in County Meath, Ireland, with prehistoric monuments including a passage tomb and earthworks. In early medieval Ireland it was the symbolic seat of the most prestigious kingship, and the title king of Tara, claimed especially by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, came to mean high king of Ireland. Its monuments include the Mound of the Hostages and the Lia Fáil, the stone of destiny.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara.jpg" alt="Hill of Tara" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paulzygmunt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Municipal_District_of_Ashbourne_-_Hill_of_Tara_-_2025-09-30_10-53-27_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Tara is an anglicisation of the Irish Teamhair, also called Teamhair na Rí, Tara of the kings. It is believed to come from a Proto-Celtic word for a sanctuary or sacred space cut off for ceremony. The remains of twenty ancient monuments are visible, and surveys have found at least three times as many. The oldest is the Mound of the Hostages, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> passage tomb built around 3,200 BCE, which held almost 300 burials in about a century and 33 more a millennium later. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> large round enclosures were built; the biggest, Ráth na Ríogh, contains the Forradh, with a standing stone believed to be the Lia Fáil, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-of-scone">Stone of Destiny</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to legend the Lia Fáil cried out when the rightful king touched it. By the start of Ireland's historical period Tara was the seat of a sacral kingship, but John T. Koch explains that no king was likely to have had enough authority to dominate the whole island before the 9th century. Tara was probably controlled by the Érainn before the Laigin seized it in the third century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a> displaced the Laigin in the fifth century, and it became the ceremonial seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>. The kingship alternated between Southern and Northern Uí Néill until the eleventh century, and the annals record a Battle of Tara in 980 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-domnaill">Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-cuaran">Amlaíb Cuarán</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a> camped on the hill during the rebellion of 1798, and British troops defeated them on 26 May. In 1843 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a> held a demonstration there for Irish self-governance that drew over 750,000 people. At the turn of the 20th century British Israelites, who believed the hill held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, dug at the Rath of the Synods, and nationalists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-hyde">Douglas Hyde</a>, Maud Gonne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">W. B. Yeats</a> protested. The Irish government bought the southern part of the hill in 1952 and the northern part in 1972. Tara is a protected national monument in the care of the Office of Public Works, which runs a visitor centre in Saint Patrick's church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hill of Tara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their sanctuaries varied: timber temples within rectangular enclosures in Gaul, Belgica and Britain, ditched enclosures called viereckschanzen in southern Germany, and circular sites in Ireland, of which the <b>Hill of Tara</b> and Navan Fort are the most notable.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Congal Cáech, king of the Ulaid and briefly claimant to the kingship of <b>Tara</b>, allied with Dál Riata but was defeated and killed by Domnall mac Áedo at Mag Rath in 637.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Irish tale The Expulsion of the Déisi tells how they were driven from Brega near <b>Tara</b> and wandered south, some settling in Munster and others crossing to Dyfed in Wales, where the Kingdom of Dyfed's kings later claimed Déisi descent.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingship of <b>Tara</b>, an ancient royal site in Meath, was claimed from the 6th century by the Ui Neill dynasties, who presented it as an ancient kingship of all Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>Brega's kings shared the kingship of <b>Tara</b> with Clann Cholmain and the northern Ui Neill, holding it often in the 7th and early 8th centuries and once more under Congalach Cnogba in the 10th.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brega">Kingdom of Brega</a></li><li><blockquote>The Laigin, the people of Leinster, appear in the earliest Irish traditions as a major power whose kings once held the midlands and <b>Tara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Kingdom of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
